  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nShower activity will continue to diminish this evening with the loss \nof daytime heating across our coastal waters, but can't rule out a \nsmall chance (10-20 percent) of a few sprinkles along our immediate \ncoast. Higher rain chances can be expected as we go into Thursday \nacross our inland counties from the Brush Country extending down to \nthe Rio Grande Plains as a result of the remnants of what is \ncurrently Tropical Depression Vance in the Eastern Pacific. The \nWeather Prediction Center(WPC) currently has our far northwestern \nCWA in a Marginal or level 1 out of 4 risk for excessive rainfall \nfor Thursday in response to a combination of enhanced lift and \nvorticity along with increasing moisture levels that is associated \nwith the remnants of Vance. The most recent ensemble model guidance \nruns and soundings shows a rather saturated profile from near the \nsurface and up through the boundary layer with PWAT values \napproaching 2.0 inches across the Brush Country, which would favor \nlocally heavy rainfall with any of the storms and localized flash \nflooding or ponding on low lying areas/roads where any training sets \nup. The rain will begin to taper off as we go into Thursday evening \nwith everyone drying out as we go into all of Friday and will \ncontinue through Halloween weekend. Our next cold front is forecast \nto arrive sometime on Tuesday of next week bring with it chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms. The best chance for showers and \nthunderstorms looks to be more towards the north and northeast \ncloser to the trough where the best upper-level dynamics will be. \nGiven the ample instability year-round across our area and the \nenhanced low to mid-level vorticity and lift with the approaching \ncold front, we cannot rule out the possibility of some strong to \nsevere thunderstorms. Trends will continue to be monitored as we get \ncloser to the event. \n\nModel Guidance does continue to show the potential for minor coastal \nflooding along the gulf facing beaches going into the weekend due to \npersistent onshore flow and elevated swells periods. There is also \nan increased risk of rip currents that will continue throughout the \nweekend.",
